# Whatever Italian translation.
#
# This file names every translatable string (`en`, like `ro` and friends)
# and doubles as the translators' template: copy it to `<lang>.po` and fill
# in `msgstr`.
#
# Conventions for translators and for code:
#
#   * `msgid` is always English and is used verbatim at the call site:
#     `tr("Restore last session", ctx: "Settings.General")`. Never
#     reword a shipped msgid; add a new entry instead, so existing
#     translations keep matching.
#   * `msgctxt` disambiguates repeats: the same English word in two places
#     (a group title vs. a menu item) gets one entry per context, named
#     `Screen.Area`, e.g. `Settings.General`.
#   * Interpolation uses `{name}` placeholders. Translators may reorder
#     or drop them freely; values are opaque strings (versions, file
#     names, counts already picked), so filling them in is pure
#     substitution and every shell does it the same way. Never add
#     printf-style verbs. Anything where the VALUE shapes the wording —
#     plural form selection by a count — uses `msgid`/`msgid_plural` plus
#     `batch` instead, evaluated core-side.
#   * Plurals use `msgid` (singular) + `msgid_plural`; the core picks the
#     form for `n` using your header's `Plural-Forms` rule.
#   * Lines starting with `#.` are developer notes for the entry below.
#   * This file is the source of truth; `whatever.mo` per language is
#     generated from `<lang>.po` at build time and never edited by hand.
msgid ""
msgstr ""
"Project-Id-Version: Whatever\n"
"Language: it\n"
"Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8\n"
"Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit\n"
"Plural-Forms: nplurals=2; plural=(n != 1);\n"

